{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Accelerating Frontier Science with a Living, Probabilistic Evidence Graph\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Authors\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi0 Joel Dietz, ...\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Created\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi0 June 19, 2026\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abstract\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rate-limiting step in frontier science is increasingly not generating data but {\i synthesizing} it: reading a flood of papers, weighing how strongly the evidence supports each hypothesis, and deciding what to test next. We describe Project Delphi, a system and methodology that accelerates this loop by maintaining a {\i living, probabilistic evidence graph} of a field. Nodes are hypotheses and the claims that sources state; edges are evidential and citation relations; and every node carries explicit, agent-attributed assessments of three kinds: a Bayesian {\i weight of evidence} in decibans, derived from reported statistics via Vovk\u8211-Sellke calibration; rubric scores assigned by a panel of language models, among them how far a claim can be tested with data that already exist; and the outcomes of tests that were actually run. The graph is versioned through time so hypotheses can be watched as they strengthen or weaken\u8212-turning \u8220"where is this field heading, and what should we measure next?\u8221" into a query rather than a literature review. Under the hood, each claim, assessment, dataset, and test result is stored as a machine-readable, independently verifiable unit (a nanopublication) that uses the Project Delphi ontology; this substrate is what makes confidence computable, auditable, and reusable, but it is a means, not the end. As of November 25, 2026, the deployed graph spans 60,410 papers, 559,764 extracted claims, and 17,075 hypotheses across 1,052 domains, and 6,182 signed nanopublications are live on the public nanopublication network, among them 2,044 claims and 3,752 model assessments. We ground the system in two case studies. In bioelectricity, claims from the Levin-lab regeneration and pattern-formation literature are weighed, and calcium-imaging measurements of injured {\i Xenopus} embryo pairs from an active wet lab are quantified so that they can enter the graph as new evidence, while the graph\u8217's confidence gaps nominate the next bench experiment. In the second, a claim from D\u8217'Arcy Thompson\u8217's {\i On Growth and Form} is taken through the whole loop on the public network: extracted with its source quote, scored for testability by two models, tested on public images, and revised twice after outside critique. We discuss how this accelerates discovery (front-and-center uncertainty, machine-actionable synthesis, experiment prioritization), its limitations (model-assisted assessment, independence assumptions, calibration as a bound), and the provenance practices required when large language models assist in evidence appraisal.\par}
