{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Frontier science advances through a loop: read what is known, weigh how strongly the evidence supports each competing hypothesis, design the experiment that would move belief the most, run it, and fold the result back in. As the literature grows\u8212-millions of papers per year, and still accelerating [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-2"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
2
}}}
]\u8212-the rate-limiting step is increasingly not generating data but {\i synthesizing} it. The bottleneck is the human loop: no one can read, weigh, and reconcile a fast-moving field in their head, so synthesis lags discovery and experiments are chosen on partial pictures. The underlying obstacle is representational. The unit of scientific communication is still the human-readable article, and the {\i claims} it makes and the strength of the {\i evidence} behind them are rarely exposed in a form that a machine\u8212-or a busy human\u8212-can query, aggregate, or track over time. This contributes to well-documented problems: difficulty synthesizing fast-moving fields, fragility of conclusions that are asserted rather than weighed, and a reproducibility landscape in which the credibility of a finding is hard to read off the literature [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-7"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
7
}}}
]. If we want to {\i accelerate} science, the loop itself\u8212-synthesis and the choice of the next experiment\u8212-is what must be made faster.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three threads of prior work point toward a remedy but have not been combined into a single, web-native, probabilistic substrate. (1) {\i Nanopublications} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-5"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
5
}}}
] make an individual assertion a first-class, citable object on the web, bundled with its provenance. (2) {\i Trusty URIs} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-10"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
10
}}}
] and decentralized nanopublication registries [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-11"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
11
}}}
] make such objects immutable, verifiable, and permanent, satisfying the FAIR principles [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-17"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
17
}}}
]. (3) A long statistical tradition\u8212-Good\u8217's {\i weight of evidence} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-3"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
3
}}}
, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-4"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
4
}}}
], Bayes factors [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-9"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
9
}}}
], and the calibration of {\i p}-values into Bayes-factor bounds [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-15"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
15
}}}
]\u8212-provides a principled scalar for \u8220"how strong is the evidence?\u8221". Scholarly knowledge graphs such as the Open Research Knowledge Graph [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-8"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
8
}}}
] structure claims, but typically without an explicit, propagating measure of evidential strength, and \u8220"living\u8221" evidence efforts [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-6"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
6
}}}
] track strength but not as a web-native graph.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Contribution.} We present Project Delphi, a system that fuses these threads:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab A {\i claim model}, the Project Delphi ontology, that keeps what a source states (a claim) apart from how credible it is (its assessments), and attaches to every claim its context, its source passage, its evidence items, and assessments with explicit agent provenance ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#the-claim-model"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 4
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Three kinds of {\i assessment}: a Bayesian calibration that converts reported statistics into per-result Bayes factors via the Vovk\u8211-Sellke bound and combines them into a single weight of evidence in decibans; rubric scores assigned by panels of language models; and executed tests against data ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#assessing-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab A {\i verifiable storage substrate} that encodes each claim, assessment, dataset, and test result as a nanopublication\u8212-canonicalized, content-addressed with a trusty URI, signed, and replicable\u8212-so that confidence is computable, auditable, and reusable rather than locked in prose ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#a-verifiable-substrate-for-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 6
}}}
), together with a dated, reproducible count of what is published ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#state-of-the-network"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 7
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab A {\i living evidence graph} that aggregates these claims and assessments into a time-versioned map of hypotheses, with confidence as an aggregate weight of evidence, and that surfaces its own gaps as an experiment-prioritization signal ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#the-living-evidence-graph"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 8
}}}
).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The emphasis throughout is on {\i accelerating the discovery loop}: the nanopublication substrate is the enabling machinery, while the contribution is a synthesis-and-prioritization layer that a lab can act on. We ground the system in two case studies: bioelectricity, where the loop reaches into an active wet lab ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#case-study-bioelectricity"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 9
}}}
), and a claim from {\i On Growth and Form} that went through the whole loop on the public network ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#case-study-a-claim-from-on-growth-and-form"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 10
}}}
). We then discuss limitations and the epistemics of model-assisted appraisal ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#discussion"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 11
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Background and Related Work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Nanopublications.} A nanopublication [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-5"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
5
}}}
] is a small RDF package with three named graphs: an {\i assertion} (the smallest unit of publishable knowledge), its {\i provenance} (how the assertion came to be), and its {\i publication information} (who minted the nanopublication, when, under what license). The form is deliberately atomic so that assertions can be cited, reused, and reasoned over independently of the paper they came from.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Verifiability and FAIR.} Trusty URIs [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-10"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
10
}}}
] embed a cryptographic hash of the content into the identifier itself, so that any later retrieval can be verified to be byte-for-byte the artifact that was minted. Combined with digital signatures and decentralized registries [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-11"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
11
}}}
], this yields knowledge objects that are Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-17"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
17
}}}
].\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Weighing evidence.} Good\u8217's {\i weight of evidence} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-3"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
3
}}}
, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-4"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
4
}}}
] is the logarithm of a Bayes factor; measured in decibans it is additive across independent evidence and aligns with human intuitions about \u8220"how many times more likely\u8221" the data are under one hypothesis than another [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-9"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
9
}}}
]. Because primary papers usually report {\i p}-values rather than Bayes factors, we use the Vovk\u8211-Sellke calibration [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-15"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
15
}}}
], which gives a defensible {\i upper bound} on the Bayes factor implied by a precise-null {\i p}-value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scholarly knowledge graphs.} The Open Research Knowledge Graph [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-8"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
8
}}}
] and living systematic reviews [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-6"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
6
}}}
] are the closest relatives. Project Delphi differs in coupling a web-native nanopublication substrate with a {\i propagating, time-versioned} weight of evidence as a first-class graph quantity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 System Overview\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#fig-delphi-pipeline"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Figure 1
}}}
 shows the end-to-end pipeline. A source paper is read (by a human or a model-assisted extractor) into one or more claims; each claim is assessed, by calibrating its reported statistics into a Bayesian weight of evidence, by scoring it on a rubric, or by running a test against data ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#assessing-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5
}}}
); claims and assessments are serialized as nanopublications, canonicalized, content-addressed, and signed; the signed nanopublications are published to a registry and ingested into the Project Delphi graph, where they become (or update) hypothesis nodes whose confidence reflects the aggregate weight of evidence and whose history is retained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1800\pich484\picwgoal8668\pichgoal2330 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Fig. 1. The Project Delphi pipeline. Papers become claims; each claim is assessed, by calibrating reported statistics into a Bayesian weight of evidence, by rubric scoring, or by an executed test; claims and assessments are published as verifiable, signed nanopublications; and they are aggregated into a time-versioned living evidence graph. New evidence flows back as a re-weighting of existing hypotheses (dashed).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 The system in deployment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Project Delphi is not only a design; it runs at corpus scale at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://scienceoracle.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
scienceoracle.com
}}}
 (earlier at probknow.com). As of November 25, 2026, its public graph holds 60,410 papers (source literature spanning 1781\u8211-2026), from which a multi-model extraction pipeline pulled 559,764 discrete claims, clustered into 17,075 hypotheses across 1,052 domains (internally called \u8220"places\u8221"). On June 19, 2026, when the first version of this paper was written, the same counts stood at 4,886 papers, 48,561 claims, 1,299 hypotheses, and 88 domains. The deployed pipeline specializes the abstract flow of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#fig-delphi-pipeline"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Figure 1
}}}
 into five stages: (1) {\i ingest} papers from OpenAlex, PubMed, and Semantic Scholar; (2) {\i extract} discrete claims and an evidence weight with a multi-LLM pipeline; (3) {\i synthesize} related claims into named, hierarchical hypotheses; (4) {\i forecast} a dated economic prediction (market size and years-to-impact) for each; and (5) {\i score} a slice with a panel of language models ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#rubric-scoring-by-model-panels"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5.2
}}}
). Coverage is uneven: 444 of the 1,052 domains have hypotheses so far, and the median domain holds 60 papers and 544 claims. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-landscape"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 1
}}}
 summarizes the graph by domain family; most of the growth since June is in domains on mathematics and its applications and on electrochemistry. Part of the project\u8217's material is published as signed nanopublications\u8212-the verifiable substrate of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#a-verifiable-substrate-for-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 6
}}}
, counted in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#state-of-the-network"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 7
}}}
\u8212-while the graph in the application holds lighter-weight nodes.\par}
