{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b An economic prioritization layer.} Beyond evidential strength, each hypothesis can carry dated economic predictions that move the question from \u8220"what is true?\u8221" toward \u8220"what is worth testing next?\u8221". Across the corpus there are 30,527 such predictions with a sized market (7,338 in June 2026), at an average of 6.4 years to achievement. Their market sizes add up to roughly $240 trillion ($45.8 trillion in June); this sum counts overlapping markets many times over and is not an estimate of total addressable value. The predictions give the experiment-prioritization signal of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#the-living-evidence-graph"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 8
}}}
 a second axis\u8212-expected economic impact\u8212-orthogonal to the evidence weight, so a lab can rank candidate experiments by both how much {\i belief} and how much {\i value} a result would move. (These forecasts are model-generated and should be read as calibrated opinion, not investment advice.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The Claim Model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The atomic unit is a {\i claim}: a discrete proposition that a source states. The graph does not assert a claim as true; its credibility is carried by the assessments attached to it. Claims, assessments, and everything around them are described with the Project Delphi ontology ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
{\f1 https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/}
}}}
, version 2.0), which reuses PROV-O, Dublin Core Terms, SKOS, DCAT, and the SPAR ontologies, among them the Citation Typing Ontology [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-14"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
14
}}}
], wherever an existing term means the same thing, and mints a term only where none does. The ontology takes the place of the {\f1 spc/1.0} schema (the \u8220"structured probabilistic claim\u8221") and the example identifiers that the first version of this paper used; it maps the terms of the earlier vocabularies to the current ones, so that nanopublications published before the change keep their meaning. Its main classes are:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Hypothesis} ({\f1 delphi:Hypothesis}) \u8212- a proposition that the graph represents without asserting it. Hypotheses form a hierarchy ({\f1 skos:broader}, {\f1 skos:narrower}), which is how a cluster of claims is tied to the proposition they bear on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Claim} ({\f1 delphi:Claim}) \u8212- a hypothesis that a source actually states. It carries a precise statement ({\f1 dcterms:description}), a short label, its kind ({\f1 dcterms:type}, for example an empirical generalization, a mechanism, a prediction, or a mathematical law), and its {\i context} ({\f1 delphi:context}): the place in the source and the system, organism, or conditions that scope it. It is tied to its source with {\f1 prov:wasDerivedFrom}, down to the quoted passage.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Testable hypothesis} ({\f1 delphi:TestableHypothesis}) \u8212- a hypothesis stated together with a proposed test ({\f1 delphi:proposedTest}), a prior probability, and a resolvability score.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Evidence item} ({\f1 delphi:EvidenceItem}) \u8212- a single piece of evidence for or against a claim ({\f1 delphi:hasEvidence}): a reported result with its statistical test and {\i p}-value, a candidate dataset, or the outcome of an executed test.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Assessment} ({\f1 delphi:Assessment}) \u8212- an evaluation of a hypothesis by an agent ({\f1 delphi:hasAssessment}), kept as a resource of its own because it relates a subject, an evaluator, a time, and a result. A {\f1 delphi:BayesianAssessment} carries a Bayes factor, a weight of evidence in decibans, and the calibration method; a {\f1 delphi:LLMClaimAssessment} carries a probability or a rubric score in [0, 1], a verdict, the assessor\u8217's confidence, and the considerations that speak against the judgement. Every assessment is attributed ({\f1 prov:wasAttributedTo}) to a software agent, which names the language model it runs ({\f1 delphi:usesModel}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Dataset} ({\f1 dcat:Dataset}) \u8212- a dataset that a test used, published once as a resource of its own with its license, source, and checksum, and referenced from the evidence item with {\f1 prov:used}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Domain} and {\b concept} ({\f1 delphi:Domain}, {\f1 delphi:Concept}) \u8212- the field a claim belongs to, and the reusable terms that claims refer to. A rubric and each of its dimensions are concepts.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This separation lets a downstream consumer take the claim, inspect its context, follow it to the sentence it came from, and read every judgement of its strength together with who made it, all without parsing prose. The ontology keeps apart two weights that are easy to confuse: {\f1 delphi:weightOfEvidenceDeciban}, the Bayesian quantity of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#bayesian-weight-of-evidence"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5.1
}}}
, and {\f1 delphi:weightOfEvidence}, an aggregate score in [0, 1] assigned by a model panel. The second is the scale of the evidence weight that the application reports, 0.83 on average over the deployed graph.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Assessing Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A claim\u8217's credibility is carried by its assessments. Project Delphi uses three kinds, which answer different questions: how strong are the statistics that the source reports ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#bayesian-weight-of-evidence"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5.1
}}}
); what does a panel of language models judge when it scores the claim against a rubric ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#rubric-scoring-by-model-panels"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5.2
}}}
); and what happened when the claim was tested against data ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#executed-tests"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5.3
}}}
). The three are kept side by side, not merged into one number, and each names the agent that produced it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Bayesian weight of evidence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We score each claim by how strongly the reported results favor the working hypothesis {\i H}{\sub 1} over a precise null {\i H}{\sub 0}. For a single result the Bayes factor is\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 $$K = \\frac\{P(\\text\{data\} \\mid H_\{1\})\}\{P(\\text\{data\} \\mid H_\{0\})\},$$\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 and the {\i weight of evidence} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-3"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
3
}}}
, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-4"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
4
}}}
] in decibans is\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\i W}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?10\u8198 ?log{\sub 10}\u8198 ?{\i K}\u8202 ?\u8193 ?[deciban].\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Primary papers usually report a {\i p}-value rather than {\i K}. The Vovk\u8211-Sellke calibration [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-15"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
15
}}}
] provides a defensible upper bound on the Bayes factor that a precise-null {\i p}-value can support,\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 $$K(p) = \\frac\{1\}\{- e\\, p\\,\\ln\\, p\},\\qquad p < e^\{- 1\},$$\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 and {\i K}({\i p}) = 1 otherwise. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-vovk-sellke"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Equation (3)
}}}
 is used conservatively: where a paper reports only a threshold (e.g. {\i p} < 0.01) we take the bound at the threshold, which {\i under}-states the evidence rather than overstating it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Assuming approximate independence across {\i n} evidence items within a claim, Bayes factors multiply and weights of evidence add:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 $$K_\{\\text\{combined\}\} = \\prod\\limits_\{i = 1\}^\{n\}K_\{i\},\\qquad W_\{\\text\{combined\}\} = \\sum\\limits_\{i = 1\}^\{n\}W_\{i\} = 10\\,\\log_\{10\}\\, K_\{\\text\{combined\}\}.$$\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The independence assumption is recorded in the assessment description so that a reader can judge it; correlated evidence inflates {\i K}{\sub combined}, so the number is best read as an optimistic, explicitly-bounded summary rather than a posterior. The additive deciban scale is what the graph layer propagates and what makes confidence comparable across claims and fields. This was the first kind of assessment the system produced: the 28 Bayesian assessments on the network were published in June 2026 ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#state-of-the-network"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 7
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Rubric scoring by model panels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The weight of evidence above answers \u8220"how strong are the {\i reported} statistics?\u8221". A complementary estimate comes from a panel of language models that assess the same claim independently of each other, so that their agreement and disagreement become visible. In June 2026 the panel consisted of up to six models (Claude Opus 4.6, GPT-5, OpenAI o3, Gemini 2.5 Pro, DeepSeek, and Grok 4.3), each of which assigned a probability that a hypothesis is true; 121 hypotheses carried such a panel, with 966 individual assessments, and Gemini was consistently the most generous scorer and Grok the most skeptical. Two things have changed since. The assessments published from October 2026 on come mainly from current models (GPT-5.6 Sol, Grok 4.7, Gemini 4 Argon, and Claude Fable 5.1, with Claude Opus 5.5 extracting the claims). And they are no longer bare probabilities but scores against a {\i rubric}: a named, versioned set of dimensions, itself published as a concept, on which a model gives a score in [0, 1] per dimension, an overall score, a verdict, its own confidence, and the considerations that speak against its judgement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two rubrics account for nearly all published assessments ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-model-panel"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 2
}}}
). {\i Dataset resolvability} does not ask whether a claim is true but whether it can be tested with data that already exist. Its five dimensions are data existence, data adequacy, operationalizability, discriminating power, and analysis feasibility; its verdict is one of {\i resolvable now}, {\i partially resolvable}, {\i needs new data}, and {\i not empirically resolvable}; and the assessor names candidate datasets and proposes a test. This rubric is what turns the graph into a queue of experiments: a claim that every model calls resolvable now can go straight to an executed test ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#executed-tests"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5.3
}}}
). {\i MorphoEval} scores how faithfully a computational model reproduces the published model of morphogenesis that it implements, on seven dimensions (source traceability, code traceability, model fidelity, parameter expressivity, morphometric validation, specimen match, and reproducibility package) and overall.\par}
