{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Table 2. Rubric assessments on the nanopublication network by model, as of November 25, 2026: number of assessments and mean overall score. The two versions of the dataset-resolvability rubric (0.1.0 and 0.2.0) are counted together. MorphoEval publishes every dimension as a nanopublication of its own (984 in all); only the overall scores are shown. Not shown are six assessments on a third rubric and 88 early MorphoEval audits that recorded their score as a probability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where the models agree, the judgement is more secure; where they diverge, the spread is itself the signal. Every one of the 1,337 claims scored for resolvability was scored by two models. On the 1,169 claims that GPT-5.6 Sol and Grok 4.7 both scored, the scores correlate strongly (Pearson {\i r} = 0.84) and differ by 0.11 on average, and the two models give the same verdict for 72% of the claims; GPT-5.6 Sol and Gemini 4 Argon, on the other 168 claims, give the same verdict for 55%. Scorer bias is systematic, as it was in June: on the same claims Grok 4.7 scores lower than GPT-5.6 Sol (0.54 against 0.62) and Gemini 4 Argon higher (0.75 against 0.64), so the gap between scorers bounds a model-disagreement uncertainty that any single scorer would hide. A rubric score remains a model\u8217's judgement and is not evidence about the world: the panel shows where models agree, not whether they are right. It does not replace the Vovk\u8211-Sellke weight of evidence or an executed test; it cross-checks the first and prepares the second, and a disagreement between a strong reported statistic and a skeptical panel is exactly the kind of claim a curator should examine first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Executed tests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The third kind of assessment is not a judgement but an outcome: a test of the claim that was actually run against data. An executed test is published as an evidence item attached to the claim. It states the prediction derived from the claim, the pass criterion, whether that criterion was fixed before the data were inspected, the measured values, and the verdict ({\i supports}, {\i mixed}, or {\i refutes}) with the tester\u8217's confidence. Every dataset the test used is published once as a nanopublication of its own, with license, source, and checksum, and the analysis steps are recorded as provenance activities with the software and model versions that ran them. When a test is criticized or repeated, the evidence item is superseded by a new version that records each revision ({\f1 prov:qualifiedRevision}): what prompted it, what was done, and the verdict and confidence before and after. Three executed tests are on the network so far, on three claims from {\i On Growth and Form}, together with five dataset nanopublications; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#case-study-a-claim-from-on-growth-and-form"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 10
}}}
 walks through one of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 A Verifiable Substrate for Claims\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the graph\u8217's confidence values to be trustworthy and reusable, each claim must be stored as an auditable, tamper-evident unit and not as re-extracted prose. We use nanopublications as that substrate. Each claim, assessment, dataset, and test result is serialized as a nanopublication in RDF/TriG with the canonical four named graphs: {\f1 Head}, {\f1 assertion}, {\f1 provenance}, and {\f1 pubinfo} ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#listing-claim-nanopub"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Listing 1
}}}
). In a claim nanopublication the assertion graph holds the claim itself: its statement, kind, label, and context. The provenance graph records where the claim came from: the source work, the quoted passage with its position in the source text and a checksum of that text, the extracting agent with the language model it runs and the person it acts for, and the plan it followed. The publication-information graph carries the creation time, creator, license, the type of the nanopublication, the resource it introduces, its domain, and the signature. An assessment is a nanopublication of its own, whose assertion graph holds the assessment and links it to the claim with {\f1 delphi:hasAssessment}, so that assessments can be added to a claim without republishing it. Identifiers live under the stable namespace {\f1 https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/}, so they resolve persistently and stay the same when a nanopublication is superseded by a newer version.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 sub:assertion \{\line
  delphir:claim/thompson1917-ch07-04 a delphi:Claim ;\line
    rdfs:label "Tissue cell walls meet three at a time at 120\u176 ?,\line
        regardless of cell size" ;\line
    dcterms:description "In sections of tissues such as plant parenchyma,\line
        as in a froth of soap-bubbles, cell walls meet three at a time at\line
        angles of 120\u176 ? whatever the sizes of the cells, while the relative\line
        sizes of neighbouring cells determine only the curvature of the\line
        walls." ;\line
    dcterms:type "mathematical-law" ;\line
    delphi:context "D\u8217'Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed.,\line
        1917), Chapter VII ... Scope: plant parenchyma, honeycomb,\line
        segmenting eggs and other cellular complexes ..." .\line
\}\line
sub:provenance \{\line
  sub:assertion prov:wasAttributedTo\line
      delphir:agent/delphisci-thompson-growth-form-extractor ;\line
    prov:wasDerivedFrom sub:quote , <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/55264> ;\line
    prov:wasGeneratedBy sub:extraction .\line
  sub:quote a prov:Entity ;\line
    prov:hadPrimarySource <https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/55264/\line
        pg55264.txt#line=10946,10950> ;\line
    schema:text "in both cases we see the cell-walls everywhere meeting, by\line
        threes, at angles of 120\u176 ?, irrespective of the size of the\line
        individual cells: ..." .\line
  delphir:agent/delphisci-thompson-growth-form-extractor\line
    a prov:SoftwareAgent ;\line
    prov:actedOnBehalfOf <https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4948-9192> ;\line
    delphi:usesModel delphir:model/claude-opus-5.5 .\line
\}\line
sub:pubinfo \{\line
  this: dcterms:created "2026-10-31T03:08:31.546Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;\line
    dcterms:creator <https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4948-9192> ;\line
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;\line
    npx:hasNanopubType delphi:Claim ;\line
    npx:introduces delphir:claim/thompson1917-ch07-04 ;\line
    delphi:domain delphir:domain/thompson-growth-form-1917 .\line
\}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Listing 1. A published claim nanopublication, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://w3id.org/np/RA-IosdDdtcLjQiGh4idcEfGXiC0VgMCJ-0fWq-VzdbnE"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
RA-IosdDdtc\u8230 ?VzdbnE
}}}
 (excerpt; the {\f1 Head} graph, the signature, and parts of the provenance are left out, and {\f1 delphi:} and {\f1 delphir:} abbreviate {\f1 https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/} and {\f1 https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/}, and one long IRI is broken across two lines). The assertion is the claim; the provenance leads to the quoted sentence and names the extracting agent and its model; the publication information declares what the nanopublication introduces.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Canonicalization, trusty URIs, signing, registries.} Before publication a claim is canonicalized (normalizing blank nodes and graph IRIs so logically identical content yields identical bytes), then assigned a {\i trusty URI} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-10"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
10
}}}
] whose suffix is a hash of that content. The nanopublication is signed with an author key bound to an ORCID, and published to a decentralized registry that replicates it [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-11"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
11
}}}
]. The result is a permanent, verifiable, independently-citable claim: anyone can re-fetch it, re-hash it, and confirm both its integrity and its authorship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 State of the Network\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is published can be counted, because every nanopublication is public and signed. This section reports a snapshot taken on November 25, 2026, through the public SPARQL service of the nanopublication network. The scripts that take the snapshot and compute every number in this section, in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-model-panel"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 2
}}}
, and in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#the-system-in-deployment"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 3.1
}}}
 are in the {\f1 analysis/} folder of this paper\u8217's repository, together with the snapshot data, so the numbers can be regenerated as the network grows. A nanopublication counts as {\i live} when its publisher has neither retracted nor superseded it. Only content is counted, not the templates, queries, and views that support it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Project Delphi nanopublications are signed by three keys. A pipeline bot published 183 between March and June 2026, in the vocabulary of that time and one statement per nanopublication. Two keys declared for the first author\u8217's ORCID iD published the rest: 1,448, all but one of them between October 13 and 26 (1,295 live), and 4,711 between October 30 and November 10 (4,704 live). In total 6,342 nanopublications were published and 6,182 are live, 97% of them from October and November 2026. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-network-kinds"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 3
}}}
 breaks the live ones down by kind and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-network-sources"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 4
}}}
 by source.\par}
