{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Table 4. Published claims and model assessments by source (domain label), as of November 25, 2026. The first seven sources were scored for dataset resolvability, and the last column counts their claims that every model judged {\i resolvable now}. The assessments of {\f1 morphogenesis} and of the sources under \u8220"Other\u8221" use MorphoEval, except for six on a third rubric.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three things stand out. First, nearly all published assessments are rubric scores by language models. The Bayesian weight of evidence appears only in the 28 assessments of June 2026. These range from 18.0 to 50.0 deciban with a median of 26.3, and 18 of the 28 carry the same value, 26.29 deciban, which is what {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-vovk-sellke"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Eqs. (3)
}}}
 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-combined"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
(4)
}}}
 give for one result at {\i p} < 0.001 combined with one at {\i p} < 0.01. Second, the resolvability rubric yields a concrete queue: of the 1,337 claims scored, the models agree that 213 (16%) can be tested with data that exist now and that 67 (5%) cannot be resolved empirically at all. Three of the 213 have been tested so far. Third, the published claims come mainly from books and from the full texts of selected papers, each claim with the quoted passage it rests on. They are a different body of material from the paper-derived graph of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#the-system-in-deployment"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 3.1
}}}
: only two of the fifteen domain labels on the network also occur among the application\u8217's domains, and joining the two is work still to be done.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The Living Evidence Graph\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Published claims are ingested into Project Delphi, a graph whose semantics are summarized in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-graph-mapping"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 5
}}}
. A node is a hypothesis or claim; an edge is an evidential, citation, or logical relation (supports, refutes, refines, depends-on); node {\i confidence} is an aggregate weight of evidence rolled up from the assessments that bear on it; and every ingest is timestamped so the graph is a versioned object. Because confidence is carried on the additive deciban scale ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-combined"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Eq. 4
}}}
), aggregation across claims and fields is a well-defined sum, and a hypothesis can be {\i watched over time}: as new nanopublications arrive, its confidence rises or falls, and the trajectory itself becomes data.\par}
