{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Table 5. Mapping from the nanopublication substrate to the living evidence graph.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Visually (as on the public Project Delphi map), node color encodes confidence and node size encodes evidence weight, and a timeline control replays how the graph for a field evolved. This turns \u8220"where is this field heading?\u8221" into a query rather than a literature review.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Case Study: Bioelectricity\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We exercise the system on bioelectricity, the study of endogenous voltage and ion-flux signals that instruct growth and form [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-12"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
12
}}}
, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-13"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
13
}}}
]. In June 2026 this was the most developed region of the deployed graph: the bioelectricity-and-regeneration theme spanned 486 papers, 119 hypotheses, and 5,745 claims, and the Levin-lab subdomain alone (labeled L1\u8211-L9) accounted for 457 papers, 112 hypotheses, and 5,513 claims. At that time it also anchored the economic layer, with the largest single predictions attached to any hypothesis, among them an AI/device combination to reverse aging (\u8764 ?$1 trillion) and pre-neural bioelectric platforms for whole-organ biofabrication (\u8764 ?$120 billion). Within this subgraph we built a focused set of claims about ion-flux control of regeneration and patterning, each with evidence linked to the primary papers by DOI and a Bayesian weight of evidence attached. The pipeline published their assessments to the network in June 2026: 28 Bayesian assessments, together with two of the claims and one evidence item ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#state-of-the-network"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 7
}}}
). Since October 2026 the network also holds 310 claims extracted from the full texts of selected Levin papers, each scored for dataset resolvability by two models; the models agree that 21 of them can be tested with data that exist now ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-network-sources"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 4
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the 28 assessments belongs to the claim that V-ATPase\u8211-driven H{\super +} flux is necessary, and a concanamycin-insensitive H{\super +} pump sufficient, for {\i Xenopus} tail regeneration [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-1"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
1
}}}
]. Four evidence items from the source were each calibrated through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-vovk-sellke"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Eq. (3)
}}}
 and combined under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-combined"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Eq. (4)
}}}
, yielding a combined Bayes factor of {\i K}{\sub combined} \u8776 ? 1.0 \u215 ? 10{\super 5} and a weight of evidence of {\i W} \u8776 ? 50.0 deciban\u8212-\u8220"decisive\u8221" on Good\u8217's scale\u8212-recorded together with the independence caveat. It is the largest of the 28 published values. One can verify the internal consistency directly from the two published statements (the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://w3id.org/np/RAEc-Yed-8gjuinzSXlJmdqiDOPylFU7wFSIZfkfGHFzI"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Bayes factor
}}}
 and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://w3id.org/np/RAKhpGo2p9kj7MPxylSTDr911vqfr63eyVi-iQwHRJYH4"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
weight of evidence
}}}
): 10\u160 ?log{\sub 10}(100588.89) = 50.03 deciban, matching the stored value. These statements are attributed to the pipeline bot; the language model that assisted the appraisal is not named in them, which the nanopublications published since October do for every assessment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Closing the loop with the wet lab\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The graph is not only fed by the {\i published} literature; in this project it is also fed by, and feeds back into, an active wet lab. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#fig-bench-graph-loop"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Figure 2
}}}
 shows the cycle. At the bench, pairs of {\i Xenopus laevis} embryos expressing a calcium reporter are imaged by time-lapse fluorescence microscopy while one embryo (\u8220"A\u8221") receives a controlled mechanical injury (a \u8220"poke\u8221"), in both direct-contact and no-contact configurations and in different head/tail orientations. The microscope emits multi-page TIFF stacks, which a quantification pipeline (the {\f1 wave-vector-analysis} tooling in the project repository) turns into numbers: it detects bright calcium \u8220"sparks\u8221" frame by frame, tracks them, segments the two embryos, assigns head/tail and anterior\u8211-posterior coordinates, maps events onto a normalized region atlas, and rolls per-frame detections up into per-cluster wave vectors ({\f1 spark_tracks.csv} \u8594 ? {\f1 vector_clusters.csv}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1800\pich827\picwgoal8668\pichgoal3982 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Fig. 2. The closed bench\u8596 ?graph loop in the bioelectricity case study. Calcium imaging of injured {\i Xenopus} embryo pairs is quantified by the project\u8217's wave-vector pipeline into measured wave properties; these become calibrated evidence nodes published as nanopublications and ingested into Project Delphi; and the graph\u8217's confidence gaps in turn nominate the next experiment. Green = wet lab, blue = instrument/compute, orange = knowledge graph.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These measurements are exactly the {\i evidence items} the claim model expects. A wet-lab observation such as \u8220"injuring embryo A raises calcium activity in both embryos\u8221" becomes a claim whose evidence is a measured post-injury activity ratio; \u8220"the response in B is a directional wave\u8221" becomes a claim backed by measured wave directions (mean \u8776 ? 5\u8211-21\u176 ?) and speeds (\u8776 ? 3\u8211-4 px s{\super \u8722 ?1}); \u8220"the local response co-locates with the wound\u8221" becomes a claim backed by a measured distance-from-poke distribution. Each such observation is calibrated ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#bayesian-weight-of-evidence"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 5.1
}}}
), serialized as a nanopublication ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#a-verifiable-substrate-for-claims"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 6
}}}
), and ingested into Project Delphi as a {\i new evidence node} attached to the corresponding literature hypothesis\u8212-the dashed re-weighting arrow of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#fig-delphi-pipeline"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Figure 1
}}}
, now realized with primary data rather than only re-read papers. The last step is still ahead: the snapshot of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#state-of-the-network"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 7
}}}
 contains no evidence item from these measurements yet.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Crucially, the graph also points back {\i toward} the bench. Because every hypothesis carries an explicit weight of evidence and an explicit set of evidence items, the graph makes its own gaps legible: some hypotheses are already well-supported, some are only {\i partially} testable with the data in hand, and some are {\i not yet testable} at all because they require conditions or metadata the current assay does not capture (for example, a graded distance series, embryo-age staging, or a contraction readout). That triage\u8212-which under-supported or untestable node would move the most evidence weight if measured next\u8212-is precisely an experiment-prioritization signal. The loop is therefore closed in both directions: bench measurements raise or lower confidence in graph hypotheses, and the graph\u8217's confidence gaps nominate the next bench experiment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Case Study: A Claim from {\i On Growth and Form}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second case study follows one claim through every step of the loop, using only what is published on the network ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#table-thompson-chain"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Table 6
}}}
). The claim comes from D\u8217'Arcy Thompson\u8217's {\i On Growth and Form} [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#ref-16"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
16
}}}
], one of the books from which claims were extracted in October 2026.\par}
