{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Table 6. The nanopublications that carry one claim from {\i On Growth and Form} through the loop. All were published between October 31 and November 1, 2026. Each is identified by its trusty code, which resolves under {\f1 https://w3id.org/np/}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Claim.} An extraction agent running Claude Opus 5.5 read the Project Gutenberg text of the first edition and published 291 claims. One of them ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#listing-claim-nanopub"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Listing 1
}}}
) is that in sections of tissues, as in a froth of soap bubbles, cell walls meet three at a time at angles of 120\u176 ? whatever the sizes of the cells. The nanopublication carries Thompson\u8217's sentence, its line range in the source file, and a checksum of that file, so a reader can check the paraphrase against the original.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Resolvability.} Half an hour later two assessors scored the claim on the dataset-resolvability rubric. GPT-5.6 Sol gave 0.89 and Grok 4.7 gave 0.81, both with the verdict {\i resolvable now}. Each named candidate datasets (the BioImage Archive; segmentations of {\i Arabidopsis} shoot meristems and an {\i Arabidopsis} embryo image collection), proposed a test (segment tissue sections, measure the angles at which walls meet and the cell sizes, and check whether triple junctions stay at 120\u176 ? independently of size), and listed what speaks against a clean result: projection and section orientation bias measured angles, and stressed or recently divided tissues depart from equilibrium.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Test.} A testing agent then ran an exploratory version of that test. It took five microscopy images from Wikimedia Commons (an aloe leaf epidermis, a {\i Drosophila} compound eye, a grass leaf epidermis, human corneal endothelium, and a moss leaflet), segmented them with Cellpose, and compared the spread of junction angles and the share of T-shaped junctions with a random Voronoi partition as control. Four of the five tissues came out closer to 120\u176 ? than the control; the grass epidermis, with 49% T-shaped junctions, did not. The verdict was {\i mixed}, with a confidence of 0.6. The evidence item says plainly what limits it: the test was not pre-registered, since the pass criterion was written after the images had been inspected, and it used one image per tissue, chosen by availability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Revisions.} The evidence item records two revisions. The first followed criticism from three outside commenters on the Moltbook forum, who are named in the nanopublication with links to their posts; one of them asked for a null in which the same statistics are run on segmenter output for synthetic packings with known junctions. That null showed a bias in the tool: the segmenter turned a brick-wall tiling with 100% T-shaped junctions into one with 3\u8211-16%, so the margin of the four conforming tissues over the control was inflated. The verdict stayed mixed and the confidence dropped from 0.6 to 0.35. The second revision brought in evidence that does not depend on that segmenter: eight expert-segmented and manually corrected {\i Drosophila} wing epithelia from two published datasets. In these, 98.4\u8211-99.7% of the interior vertices are three-way, so the \u8220"by threes\u8221" half of the claim holds. The angles tell a different story. In dividing wing discs only 16\u8211-21% of the junctions have all three angles within 15\u176 ? of 120\u176 ?, close to the random control (14%), and in pupal wings that share rises from 24\u8211-27% to 42\u8211-49% over the 17 hours in which division ceases. Junction angles therefore approach 120\u176 ? as a tissue leaves proliferation, not \u8220"irrespective of size\u8221" as a standing property. The verdict remained mixed and the confidence returned to 0.6, now resting on expert segmentations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b What the case shows.} The loop closed within two days and with public data only, and every step is a signed, citable object: a reader can go from Thompson\u8217's sentence to the measured values and back. Criticism became part of the record, and confidence moved down and up again in response to it. The outcome is a refinement, not a verdict of true or false: one sentence splits into a part that holds and a part that depends on the state of the tissue. The case also shows what is still missing. The test is exploratory and small; the extraction, the scoring, and the test were all run by agents of one author; and the nanopublications point to the scripts that produced them at fixed commits of a repository that is not public at the time of writing, so a reader cannot yet rerun the analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b How this accelerates discovery.} The payoff is a faster discovery loop. Synthesis that previously required reading and mentally reconciling a field becomes a query over a graph whose node confidences are explicit and comparable; uncertainty is front-and-center rather than buried in a results section; and because the graph is time-versioned, the {\i dynamics} of belief are observable rather than reconstructed after the fact. Most directly, the graph\u8217's confidence gaps act as an experiment-prioritization signal, shortening the path from \u8220"what is known\u8221" to \u8220"what to measure next.\u8221" That claims also become verifiable, citable, machine-readable objects is what makes this computable\u8212-but it is the means, not the goal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Model-assisted appraisal and provenance.} In our corpus the Bayesian assessments were produced with the assistance of a large language model, as were claim extraction, rubric scoring, and test execution. We regard transparent agent attribution as a requirement, not an option: a reader must be able to see that an appraisal was model-assisted, which model, and under what stated assumptions, so that it can be audited or redone. The nanopublications of June 2026 fall short of this, since they are attributed to the pipeline bot without naming the model ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#case-study-bioelectricity"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Section 9
}}}
). Every claim, assessment, dataset, and test result published since October 30, 2026, names the software agent ({\f1 prov:wasAttributedTo}), the language model it runs, and the person it acts for. Once published, this provenance is immutable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Limitations.} (1) The Vovk\u8211-Sellke value is a {\i bound}, not a posterior; reported strengths are optimistic by construction and should be read as upper limits. (2) The independence assumption in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#eq-combined"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Eq. (4)
}}}
 is frequently violated by multiple readouts of one experiment; we mitigate by recording it, but correlated evidence inflates the combined factor. (3) Claim extraction and calibration are only as good as the (possibly model-assisted) reader, motivating the strict provenance above. (4) Registries deliberately do not support bulk upload, so publishing at scale requires either rate-limited batching or operating one\u8217's own registry node.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Effect size, not just significance.} A weight of evidence derived from a {\i p}-value answers \u8220"how surprising are these data under the null?\u8221" but is silent on \u8220"how large is the effect?\u8221"\u8212-and at the small sample sizes typical of frontier experiments the two can diverge sharply. We therefore pair each calibrated {\i p}-value with a nonparametric effect-size estimate (Cliff\u8217's {\i \u948 ?} for two-group contrasts, the matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation for paired designs), so that a claim node carries both a strength-of-evidence and a magnitude. This is more than bookkeeping: the combination defines a useful triage state for the wet lab. A {\i large effect that is not yet significant}\u8212-a real-looking signal that is simply underpowered\u8212-is exactly the confidence gap the graph should flag as a high-value next experiment, whereas a {\i significant but negligible} effect (common with large {\i n}) warns against over-promoting a claim. In the bioelectricity case study these effect sizes are computed with nonparametric, paired, and batch-aware tests appropriate to small, non-normal, clutch-clustered calcium-imaging data.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Future work.} Principled handling of dependent evidence; community curation and counter-nanopublications that {\i refute} or {\i refine} existing claims; calibration against held-out replication outcomes; carrying effect-size magnitude alongside the weight of evidence as a second confidence coordinate that drives experiment prioritization; and richer edge semantics for mechanistic chains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We described Project Delphi, a system and methodology for {\i accelerating frontier science} by maintaining a living, probabilistic evidence graph that synthesizes a field and prioritizes its next experiments. Credibility is carried by explicit, agent-attributed assessments: an additive Bayesian weight of evidence, rubric scores from model panels, and the outcomes of executed tests. The graph is versioned so that belief can be watched as it evolves; and a verifiable nanopublication substrate\u8212-the enabling machinery, not the headline\u8212-makes that confidence computable, auditable, and reusable. A bioelectricity case study shows the loop reaching into an active wet lab, from primary measurement to calibrated evidence node to a prioritized next experiment, and a claim from {\i On Growth and Form} shows it closed on the public network, from a source sentence to a tested and twice-revised result. The aim is a literature one does not only read, but can query, weigh, watch\u8212-and act on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Reproducibility.} The ontology, the nanopublication templates, and the publishing tooling are available in the project repository ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/fractastical/project-delphi-ontology"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
github.com/fractastical/project-delphi-ontology
}}}
); the nanopublications themselves are on the public nanopublication network, each independently verifiable through its trusty URI; and the scripts and snapshot data behind the counts in this paper are in the {\f1 analysis/} folder of its repository ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/knowledgepixels/living-evidence-paper"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
github.com/knowledgepixels/living-evidence-paper
}}}
).\par}
